# Line endings
#
# Kotlin, Gradle, YAML and shell sources are stored with LF and checked out with CRLF on
# Windows, so a build script edited on either platform produces the same blob and the
# no-INTERNET gate cannot be defeated by a line-ending change to a manifest.
* text=auto eol=crlf

*.kt        text eol=crlf
*.kts       text eol=crlf
*.java      text eol=crlf
*.xml       text eol=crlf
*.yml       text eol=crlf
*.yaml      text eol=crlf
*.sh        text eol=crlf
*.md        text eol=crlf
*.py        text eol=crlf
*.txt       text eol=crlf
*.pro       text eol=crlf
*.properties text eol=crlf

# Binary artifacts. Marked explicitly rather than left to Git's extension guessing.
#
# These must never be run through a text filter. A .docx and a .wav are ZIP and RIFF
# containers whose integrity depends on exact bytes; normalising line endings inside one
# produces a file that still looks like a ZIP and is no longer readable by Word or by
# anything else. `text=auto` alone is not enough, because it inspects content and a
# binary blob can contain byte sequences that look like text.
*.docx      binary
*.onnx      binary
*.wav       binary
*.apk       binary
*.jar       binary
*.aar       binary
*.keystore  binary

# The speech model, via Git LFS.
#
# WHY LFS
# -------
# `base-decoder.int8.onnx` is 130,672,026 bytes. GitHub rejects any blob over 100 MiB, so
# committing it directly makes the push fail outright -- this is a hard limit, not a warning
# that can be accepted. Git LFS stores the content out of line and leaves a small pointer in
# the commit, which keeps the working tree identical (Gradle reads the same files either way)
# while making the repository cloneable.
#
# The alternative -- not committing the model at all -- was considered and rejected: it makes
# a fresh clone unable to build an app that can actually dictate, which is the one thing this
# project is about. The cost is that cloning now requires git-lfs, which `BUILD.md` states.
#
# `base-tokens.txt` and the test .wav are deliberately NOT tracked through LFS. At 817 KB and
# 352 KB they belong in ordinary git history, where they can be read, diffed and reviewed
# without an LFS client, and the tokens file is small enough that a reviewer can verify its
# integrity by eye against the SHA-256 in the models README.
*.onnx filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# The tokens file must stay byte-identical to the model it belongs to.
#
# DO NOT REMOVE THIS, and do not let a general rule swallow it.
#
# The `*.txt text eol=crlf` rule above is correct for prose and catastrophic here. This file has
# 50,257 lines, so CRLF checkout rewrites it from 816,730 bytes to 866,987 -- one added byte
# per line. `ModelStore` verifies this file's SHA-256 before it will build a recogniser, so
# every fresh clone would hash-mismatch, discard the model as corrupt, and silently have no
# speech engine at all. The failure would not look like a line-ending problem; it would look
# like the model failing.
#
# Caught by cloning the published repository and comparing sizes, which is the only check that
# would have found it: the file is valid text, so nothing was wrong in the working copy that
# produced it. `-text` disables all end-of-line conversion, so the blob is checked out exactly
# as committed.
app/src/main/assets/models/whisper-base/base-tokens.txt -text

# Belt and braces: nothing under the model directory is text.
app/src/main/assets/models/** -text